From 3990e7818841092a7755bac4761c2f67d0299e76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: knfsd-fuzz <knfsd-fuzz@localhost>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 20:20:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] kcov: attribute remote coverage to per-program request
 generations

Remote KCOV identifies the collector only by its common handle.  An executor
reuses one handle for every program it runs, so work that finishes after a
program ends is recorded into the next program, and nothing tells a server
thread whether the request it handles still belongs to an open program.

Add request generations.  An executor opens a generation per program with
KCOV_REMOTE_GENERATION_BEGIN and closes it with FINISH (bounded drain) or
ABORT.  Remote work is admitted only through a token minted from a root of
the generation that is still open (kcov_request_root_init(),
kcov_request_try_get_child_token(), kcov_request_remote_try_grant()), and is
collected in a private scratch area that is merged into the generation's
aggregate.  FINISH publishes the aggregate into the executor's remote area,
so coverage from a closed, aborted or different generation is never mixed
in.  Continuation tokens carry an attribution into deferred and asynchronous
work.

Within one remote section repeated events for the same PC are suppressed so
that the scratch area measures distinct coverage rather than execution
count.  kcov_remote_start_nfsd_observe() lets the NFS server transport record
its pre-dispatch stages into a separate global observation handle that is
never merged into a generation.

kcov_request_current_generation() returns the generation currently open for
a common handle; the SUNRPC lane attribution uses it to own server work.

debugfs (under sunrpc_fuzz/) exposes generation, admission and aggregation
counters.
