diff --git a/Documentation/dev-tools/kcov.rst b/Documentation/dev-tools/kcov.rst
index 1a739290c..0cfae30da 100644
--- a/Documentation/dev-tools/kcov.rst
+++ b/Documentation/dev-tools/kcov.rst
@@ -383,3 +383,59 @@ local tasks spawned by the process and the global task that handles USB bus #1:
 		perror("close"), exit(1);
 	return 0;
     }
+
+NFSD stage-presence observation (downstream NF-A1)
+------------------------------------------------
+
+``KCOV_SUBSYSTEM_NFSD_OBSERVE`` is ``0x02ull << 56``. Its only instance is 1:
+``KCOV_NFSD_OBSERVE_HANDLE == 0x0200000000000001ull``. This is one global,
+best-effort observation stream across NFSD workers, sockets and network
+namespaces. It does not establish request ownership, ordering, or a causal
+connection between PCs from different stages.
+
+The guest collector opens a separate KCOV descriptor, uses ``KCOV_INIT_TRACE``
+and ``mmap`` as above, clears the count before enabling, then supplies::
+
+    trace_mode    = KCOV_TRACE_PC
+    area_size     = scratch size in unsigned long words (at least 3)
+    num_handles   = 1
+    common_handle = 0
+    handles[0]    = KCOV_NFSD_OBSERVE_HANDLE
+
+``area_size`` must also fit the existing KCOV size limit. Three words suffice
+for the scratch-pool header on both 32-bit and 64-bit builds; practical buffers
+should be much larger. The mmap size is independently set by ``KCOV_INIT_TRACE``.
+Softirq sections use the preallocated ``CONFIG_KCOV_IRQ_AREA_SIZE`` buffers,
+not ``area_size``. Comparison mode, mixed handles, a common handle, other
+instances, reserved handle bits and undersized scratch are rejected with
+``EINVAL``. A second collector of this global handle receives ``EEXIST``.
+Do not use the request-generation ioctls on this descriptor.
+
+The annotated stages are NFSD's actual softirq ``svc_data_ready`` callback
+(including its ``svc_xprt_enqueue`` call), the dequeue portion of ``svc_recv``
+(ending before ``svc_handle_xprt``). The observer does not wrap
+``svc_process``: existing checked request tickets cover that stage after
+managed admission. Global observation never substitutes for request-attributed
+output. Task-context data-ready callbacks are deliberately not observed by
+this hook.
+
+``kcov_remote_start_nfsd_observe()`` returns true only after installing a private
+scratch buffer. Its caller invokes ``kcov_remote_stop()`` exactly once, in the
+same context, only after true. Unsupported contexts (including atomic task
+context), an attached managed ticket, incumbent KCOV, absent registration or
+unavailable scratch all cause a passive false return. It does not suspend an
+incumbent, invalidate a managed generation or issue nesting warnings. The
+original ``kcov_remote_start()`` API and the sequence-checked remote merge
+remain unchanged.
+
+For a stable snapshot, the enabling thread calls ``KCOV_DISABLE`` before reading
+the mapped count with acquire semantics and consuming that many PCs. Disable
+removes the handle; sections still in flight are discarded by the existing
+sequence check rather than drained. Reset the count only while disabled. Buffer
+exhaustion and skipped sections mean missing PCs are not evidence that a stage
+did not execute. Observed PCs are stage-presence evidence only, never proof that
+one request traversed all stages. No new overflow or completeness ABI is added.
+
+``CONFIG_KCOV_SELFTEST`` includes deterministic registration-policy, passive
+rejection, private-scratch, nesting, merge and stale-sequence checks for this
+observer. Running those boot tests is separate from compiling the patch.
